<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Antoninus Pius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">86–161</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Antoninus Pius was Roman emperor from 138 to 161, adopted heir of Hadrian and adoptive father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a>. His long reign was among the most peaceful in Roman history; he never left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> as emperor. The one major advance of the frontier was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, where his governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-lollius-urbicus">Quintus Lollius Urbicus</a> built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonine-wall">Antonine Wall</a> between the Forth and the Clyde.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antoninus-pius.jpg" alt="Antoninus Pius" width="320" height="481" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jebulon · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antoninus_Pius_bust_S2436_ancient_agora_museum_Athens.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Antoninus was born Titus Aurelius Fulvus Boionius Antoninus in 86, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lanuvium">Lanuvium</a>, the son of Titus Aurelius Fulvus, consul in 89, and Arria Fadilla. His family was a relatively new senatorial house from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-narbonensis">Gallia Narbonensis</a>, supported by the Flavians. After his father's early death he was raised by his maternal grandfather Gnaeus Arrius Antoninus, a friend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-younger">Pliny the Younger</a>.</p><p>Between 110 and 115 he married Annia Galeria Faustina the Elder. He became consul in 120, administered part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> as one of four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/proconsul">proconsuls</a>, and was proconsul of Asia, probably in 134 and 135. On 25 February 138 Hadrian adopted him, on condition that he adopt Marcus Annius Verus and Lucius, son of Lucius Aelius, later the emperors <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-verus">Lucius Verus</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Antoninus made few changes at first, and his ruling team centred on a group of conservative senatorial families. He persuaded the Senate to grant divine honours to Hadrian, which is the most likely reason for the title Pius. J. J. Wilkes notes that he probably never commanded a Roman army and was never within five hundred miles of a legion in his twenty-three years. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> the governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-lollius-urbicus">Quintus Lollius Urbicus</a> invaded southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonine-wall">Antonine Wall</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firth-of-forth">Firth of Forth</a> to the Firth of Clyde; it was decommissioned from the mid-150s and abandoned in the early 160s, for reasons that remain unclear.</p><p>He left a treasury of about 2.7 billion sesterces, suspended taxes in cities hit by fire and earthquake, and gave grants to rebuild Greek cities after earthquakes in about 140 and in 152. He is also credited with dividing imperial property into the patrimonium and the res privata.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a>, consul with Antoninus in 140, took on more duties as the emperor aged. Antoninus died in 161, probably on 7 March, shortly after Marcus and Lucius were designated joint consuls. The Antonine Plague, brought back by soldiers after the Parthian victory, soon depleted the treasury. The only complete ancient account of his life, the Historia Augusta, is unreliable and largely fabricated, though it is the sole source to mention the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonine-wall">Antonine Wall</a>. Edward Gibbon and others praised him as an ideal landed gentleman, while some historians take a less favourable view. Only one of his four children reached adulthood, but he was ancestor of four generations of prominent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, including the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commodus">Commodus</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Antoninus Pius” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…route from Malaysia to central Vietnam. Óc Eo, its port and entrepôt, has yielded Roman, Persian, Indian and Greek artefacts: Louis Malleret found there in the 1940s gold medallions of <b>Antoninus Pius</b> and Marcus Aurelius and locally made gold discs imitating coins of Antoninus, Commodus and Septimius Severus.</blockquote><a href="/empire/funan">Funan · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>After Pompey’s campaign Iberia became a client of Rome, and King Pharasmanes II visited Rome, probably under <b>Antoninus Pius</b>, where he was received with honour and allowed to sacrifice on the Capitol.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-iberia">Kingdom of Iberia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The first important expansion came late in the reign of <b>Antoninus Pius</b> and under Marcus Aurelius, and the cult reached the height of its popularity in the 2nd and 3rd centuries, spreading at an “astonishing” rate.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mithraism">Mithraism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>On the death of <b>Antoninus Pius</b>, Marcus Aurelius succeeded and made his adoptive brother Lucius Verus co-emperor.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-7">7 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lanuvium">Lanuvium</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Lorium, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.68,12.7&amp;year=134" aria-label="Lanuvium on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4859 679 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Antoninus Pius"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5309 949h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5309 949h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.68,12.7&amp;year=134">What was here in 134 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
